Following the observed behavior of the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay, a shape change in the differential decay rate is predicted for the $B_d^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}}$ decay. Both form factors involved in these decays are computed using lattice QCD at a high resolution of 1%. By combining the lattice data with experimental measurements of $B_d^0$ and $B_s^0$ mass differences, the form factors are predicted over an extensive range of energy transfers. While the shapes of the form factor curves are found to be similar, the curve for the axial-vector form factor is significantly lower due to the mass difference between the bottom and charm quarks. The $B_d^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay rate is primarily influenced by this disparity in energy transfer curves, resulting in a similar shape change to the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay rate.
